    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 1 (Questions 1&ndash;6)</div>\n<p>In his landmark 1972 study <i>The Columbian Exchange</i> and its 1986 sequel <i>Ecological Imperialism</i>, historian Alfred Crosby effected a profound paradigm shift in the historiography of European expansion. For generations, conventional Eurocentric narratives attributed the rapid subjugation of the Americas to cultural superiority, religious providence, or decisive technological advantages such as ocean-going caravels, firearms, and steel weaponry. Crosby dismantled this triumphalist orthodoxy by demonstrating that European conquest was fundamentally a biological phenomenon. The reconnection of previously insulated hemispheric biotas, severed since the retreat of the Beringia land bridge, unleashed an unprecedented ecological and epidemiological revolution. Old World pathogens\u2014most notably smallpox, measles, typhus, and influenza\u2014swept through indigenous populations that possessed zero immunological memory, triggering catastrophic &ldquo;virgin-soil epidemics.&rdquo; Within a century of initial transatlantic contact, these contagious infections decimated indigenous American societies by an estimated eighty to ninety-five percent, dismantling complex imperial polities like the Aztec and Incan states before sustained military conquest had even commenced.</p>\n<p>Yet this biological globalization was not confined to microbial contagion; it entailed a sweeping, systemic reconfiguration of the American physical environment. Alongside pathogens, European colonizers introduced an aggressive vanguard of domesticated animals and invasive flora that encountered vast ecological niches devoid of natural predators or specialized competitors. Crosby coined the term &ldquo;ecological imperialism&rdquo; to delineate how introduced Old World species actively paved the way for European settlement. Prolific herds of swine, cattle, and sheep multiplied exponentially across the grasslands of the Pampas and the North American plains, transforming delicate indigenous polycultures into neo-European pastoral terrain. Feral pigs devoured native root crops and ravaged fragile riparian habitats, while roaming cattle trampled sophisticated indigenous irrigation networks and accelerated severe soil erosion. This environmental degradation undermined native subsistence strategies, exacerbating the physical dislocation caused by epidemic mortality and eroding indigenous communities' capacity to mount organized resistance.</p>\n<p>Despite its profound explanatory power, Crosby&rsquo;s framework has provoked important critical revisions from contemporary ethnohistorians and indigenous studies scholars. Critics argue that Crosby&rsquo;s early formulation flirted with an uncritical biological determinism. By elevating pathogens to the status of primary historical actors, early Columbian Exchange historiography risked depoliticizing colonial conquest, framing catastrophic indigenous depopulation as an accidental, blameless natural tragedy. Revisionist scholars emphasize that virgin-soil epidemics did not strike populations in an institutional vacuum. Pathogens operated synergistically with the institutional violence of colonial rule: forced labor in silver mines, brutal chattel enslavement, compulsory Christian resettlement into crowded mission hamlets, and the calculated destruction of native food stores. Starvation, psychological trauma, and physical exhaustion drastically suppressed immune function, transforming infections that might otherwise have been survivable into lethal, society-ending conflagrations. Microbes were lethal, but colonial brutality transformed biological vulnerability into near-total demographic collapse.</p>\n<p>Finally, the Columbian Exchange was characterized by a profound global asymmetry that generated a historical paradox. While the Western Hemisphere experienced catastrophic demographic collapse, the transfer of New World domesticates revolutionized global caloric availability across Eurasia and Africa. Staple cultigens such as potatoes, maize, cassava, and sweet potatoes produced significantly more calories per acre than traditional Old World grains like wheat, rye, and barley, while flourishing in rocky or moisture-deficient soils. The widespread adoption of these nutrient-dense cultigens mitigated chronic famine, fueling an unprecedented population boom across eighteenth-century Europe and Qing-dynasty China. In a bitter historical irony, the demographic explosion that provided the indispensable labor force for European industrialization and imperial expansion was directly underwritten by crops salvaged from the ruin of the very civilizations European colonization had annihilated.</p>",
