    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 2 (Questions 7&ndash;12)</div>\n<p>In her influential work <i>Along the Archival Grain: Epistemic Anxieties and Colonial Common Sense</i> (2009), anthropologist and historian Ann Laura Stoler mounted a decisive intervention in the historiography of European imperialism. For over a century, conventional historians approached colonial archives as empirical repositories of objective documentation&mdash;inert storehouses of records to be mined for factual data regarding subject populations, taxation revenues, and judicial rulings. Drawing upon post-structuralist insights regarding the entanglement of knowledge and political power, Stoler radically inverts this methodological posture. She demonstrates that the colonial archive is not a transparent window onto past events, nor a neutral ledger of administrative governance, but an active, epistemological technology of rule. Bureaucratic paper trails&mdash;censuses, legal statutes, commission inquiries, and administrative correspondence&mdash;did not simply record pre-existing colonial realities; they actively produced them, fabricating the racial taxonomies, moral hierarchies, and legal boundaries essential to sustaining imperial domination.</p>\n<p>Central to Stoler&rsquo;s paradigm is the critical distinction between reading archival documents <i>against</i> the grain and reading <i>along</i> it. Influenced by Subaltern Studies, social historians traditionally read colonial archives against the grain, probing between bureaucratic margins to recover the suppressed voices, everyday resistances, and political agency of colonized subjects who were excluded from formal records. While recognizing the moral and political imperative of this recovery work, Stoler advocates reading along the archival grain: interrogating the administrative apparatus itself. Reading along the grain entails tracing the anxieties, uncertainties, rumors, and hesitations that permeated colonial bureaucracy. Rather than revealing a monolithic, rational, and omniscient imperial machine executing a coherent master plan, the archival paper trail reveals an imperial state perpetually haunted by its own vulnerability. Official dossiers are filled with intense anxieties regarding European moral degeneracy, racial contamination, and the precariousness of colonial authority in the face of local resistance.</p>\n<p>This administrative anxiety was particularly acute at the intimate frontiers of empire: the domestic sphere, sexual cohabitation, and child-rearing. Examining the nineteenth- and early-twentieth-century archives of the Dutch East Indies and French Indochina, Stoler reveals how imperial authorities obsessively regulated intimate arrangements, particularly concubinage between European men and native women, and the legal status of their mixed-race offspring. Far from being trivial domestic matters subordinate to grand statecraft, the management of intimacy was deemed foundational to state security. Imperial officials harbored deep anxieties that unacknowledged mixed-race youths and impoverished, &ldquo;poor white&rdquo; European settlers would blur the rigid racial boundaries between ruler and ruled, erode the prestige of whiteness, and forge political allegiances with the indigenous populace. Consequently, colonial administrations constantly revised paternity laws, residency permits, and educational curricula to police racial boundaries, demonstrating that racial classifications were not biological realities, but fragile, continuously contested political fabrications.</p>\n<p>Subaltern historians, however, have articulated significant reservations regarding Stoler&rsquo;s methodological pivot. They caution that an exclusive focus on reading along the archival grain risks recentering European subjectivity, once again relegating the colonized to the periphery of historical inquiry as mere shadows that appear only when they provoked colonial apprehension. Furthermore, empiricist critics argue that official paperwork may reflect the rhetorical self-dramatization of bureaucrats seeking funding from metropolitan ministries rather than actual governance on the ground. Nonetheless, Stoler&rsquo;s scholarship permanently dismantles the presumption of archival neutrality. By transforming the archive from an unproblematic source of empirical evidence into an object of historical inquiry in its own right, she exposes colonial rule as an anxious, improvisational project defined by its persistent struggle to manage the internal contradictions of imperial authority.</p>",
