    "passage": "<div class=\"ttl\">Passage 3 (Questions 13&ndash;18)</div>\n<p>In the historiography of modern American foreign relations, few works have exerted a more seismic influence than William Appleman Williams&rsquo;s 1959 classic, <i>The Tragedy of American Diplomacy</i>. In the immediate aftermath of the Second World War, orthodox American scholarship&mdash;represented by eminent historians such as Arthur Schlesinger Jr., Herbert Feis, and George F. Kennan&mdash;codified a comforting national narrative. In this orthodox consensus, the Cold War was portrayed as a reluctant, defensive American response to unprovoked Soviet aggression and messianic totalitarian expansionism. The United States, having dismantled its wartime military apparatus in good faith, was cast as an isolationist republic forced onto the global stage solely to preserve democratic civilization against Stalinist despotism. Williams and the &ldquo;Wisconsin School&rdquo; of revisionist history shattered this consensus, arguing that postwar American foreign policy was not an ad hoc, reactive defense of liberty, but the natural culmination of a long-standing, bipartisan pursuit of global commercial hegemony dating back to the late nineteenth century.</p>\n<p>At the center of Williams&rsquo;s interpretive framework was the ideology of the &ldquo;Open Door.&rdquo; Originating in Secretary of State John Hay&rsquo;s diplomatic notes of 1899&ndash;1900 regarding access to Chinese markets, the Open Door transcended specific commercial treaties to become the operating system of American foreign policy. Williams argued that American policymakers operated under a shared intellectual paradigm: domestic capitalist prosperity, social stability, and democratic political institutions depended upon an ever-expanding overseas economic frontier. Confronting recurrent cycles of industrial depression and domestic surplus production, political and business elites concluded that severe economic stagnation and class conflict could be avoided only by securing unhindered international markets for American goods and surplus capital. Crucially, this did not require formal colonial annexation; rather, Washington favored an informal empire of free trade, compelling foreign nations to lower tariff walls and embrace open financial systems under American commercial dominance.</p>\n<p>This structural economic imperative, Williams maintained, transformed American diplomacy into an aggressive engine of geopolitical confrontation. In the post-1945 landscape, Washington sought to universalize this Open Door world order through the Bretton Woods institutions, the World Bank, and the International Monetary Fund. However, this globalist architecture collided directly with Soviet national security imperatives. Having suffered over twenty-seven million casualties during the Nazi invasion, the Soviet Union insisted on establishing an exclusive security buffer of client states across Eastern Europe. When Moscow closed Eastern Europe to Western trade and capital investment, American leaders did not perceive this as defensive geopolitical posturing; rather, they interpreted it as an intolerable, existential assault on the universal principles of the Open Door. Conflating the global expansion of capitalism with the preservation of freedom itself, Washington treated any barrier to international trade as an aggressive act of totalitarian containment.</p>\n<p>Post-revisionist scholars, notably John Lewis Gaddis, subsequently criticized the Wisconsin School for succumbing to an overly rigid economic determinism. Gaddis argued that Williams minimized Joseph Stalin&rsquo;s acute paranoia, brutal internal purges, and genuine territorial expansionism, while underestimating the degree to which Western European democracies voluntarily solicited American financial and military intervention through the Marshall Plan and NATO. Yet Williams&rsquo;s revisionist thesis remains indispensable because its critique was fundamentally tragic and moral rather than cynical or conspiratorial. Williams did not view American statesmen as corrupt imperialists; rather, they were tragic captives of a pervasive national mythology. Convinced that their own economic model was synonymous with universal human progress, American leaders persistently interpreted indigenous national liberation movements and foreign socialist developments as existential threats, subverting democratic self-determination in the very name of defending global liberty.</p>",
